{{- /*
  support/ask-escalation — owner-ask step for the `escalation.md` fragment.
  Owner: `Support: investigate`.

  Rendered by the owner prompt when
  `.mitto/support/<channel-id>/escalation.md` does not yet exist. Instructs
  the agent to open a focused `mitto_ui_form` covering only escalation
  paths (who/when/how to escalate, human hand-off contacts), then write the
  file.

  Fields on the `dict` argument:

    TimeoutSeconds:  int. Interactive owners pass 300; silent owners a
                     short value.

  Whitespace: fragment body starts flush at column 1 (no leading `- `) and
  emits no trailing newline; callers use plain `{{ template ... }}`.
*/ -}}
{{- $timeout := .TimeoutSeconds -}}
**Ask + write `escalation.md`.** Open a focused `mitto_ui_form`
  (`timeout_seconds: {{ $timeout }}`) titled "Channel escalation — who do we escalate to, and
  when?" with these fields (all required unless noted):
  - `who` (textarea) — "Human hand-off contacts: names, Slack handles, or team channels for each
    kind of question. Be specific — 'auth issues → @sec-oncall in #sec-help', not 'ask security'."
  - `when` (textarea) — "When to escalate: what signals mean we should stop investigating and hand
    off (severity, blast radius, customer tier, elapsed time)."
  - `how` (textarea, optional) — "How to escalate: DM vs @-mention vs opening a ticket; any SLA or
    on-call rotation link."
  - `notes` (textarea, optional) — "Anything else relevant to escalation (holiday coverage, backup
    contacts, edge cases)."

  When the form returns, create `.mitto/support/<channel-id>/` if it does not exist, then write the
  result to `.mitto/support/<channel-id>/escalation.md` as a Markdown document with sections
  `# Who`, `# When`, `# How` (only if non-empty), and `# Notes` (only if non-empty). Confirm the
  write via `mitto_ui_notify` (success). If the form times out or is aborted, do **not** write a
  partial file — fall back per the caller's policy.
{{- /* no trailing newline */ -}}
